## Round 1

? Mina Owczyńska [off-CHIN-ska], mother of Romain Gary, believed her son was a genius writer and begged him never to take part in THIS. Identify THIS using one word of Medieval Latin origin.
! Duel(s).
/ Gary’s mother was born in the Russian Empire and believed that her son was equal in talent to Pushkin and Lermontov, and so she begged him never to fight in a duel, to avoid dying in the bloom of his literary career as they did.
^ 
- https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Romain_Gary
- Р. Гари. Обещание на заре (Romain Gary, La Promesse de l'aube); https://tinyurl.com/2hjf36ey
- https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/duel#Etymology
@ Maxim Merzlyakov

? Handout:
(img 2022-2023_V_en_001.png)
Steven Pinker compared the attempts to teach apes human language to trying to force the animal on the picture to pick things up with its nostrils. These attempts were guided solely by the fact that its closest living relative is WHO?
! Elephant(s) [also accept Proboscidea(ns)].
/ Two feet in length and looking like a guinea pig, the hyrax is the species closest related to elephants. Pinker compares language to the elephant’s trunk, another unique development that has no equivalent in any other extant animal. Pinker says that if researchers were as fixated on trunks as they are on language, they would conclude that the hyrax has one, just because it has nostrils.
^ 
- https://tinyurl.com/yc73d95j
- С. Пинкер. Язык как инстинкт (Steven Pinker, The Language Instinct); https://tinyurl.com/4mstc8s6
- https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Hyrax
@ Maxim Merzlyakov

? The Russian word for “arsenic” is linked not to bats, but to rodents. Nevertheless, in folklore the mineral arsenopyrite was described as a magic amulet, because it produces THIS when struck. Identify THIS.
! The smell of garlic [accept any answer that mentions smell and garlic].
/ Because of its garlic smell, arsenopyrite was believed to be a magic amulet warding off undead, such as vampires, who are commonly associated with bats in legends. In fact, the mineral can keep away or poison rodents as well as bats, hence the Russian name.
^ 
- https://ru.wikipedia.org/wiki/Мышьяк
- https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Arsenopyrite
- https://tinyurl.com/3jrk6kmf
@ Maxim Merzlyakov

? In the human body, THEY suppress the activity of platelets, while in another animal host, conversely, they block the production of an anti-coagulant. Both effects improve the probability of THEIR spread. Identify THEM.
! malaria parasite(s) [accept plasmodium/a, malaria germ]
/ Platelets are responsible for blood clotting, and the malaria parasite affects them to facilitate feeding for blood-sucking mosquitoes who will then transmit the disease to new hosts. In the mosquito's body the parasite’s action is reverse: it blocks the production of a blood-thinning enzyme. The mosquito becomes more thirsty and bites more humans, increasing the probability of the parasite spreading. Parasites are very ingenious.
^ К. Циммер. Паразит — царь природы (Carl Zimmer, Parasite Rex); https://tinyurl.com/2cdx4baf
@ Maxim Merzlyakov

? British scientist John Haldane would often accompany his father to a mine, and once narrowly escaped death in a methane bubble. Later he acquired a habit that made his friends afraid of standing close to him. Using another person remembered by the writer Bill Mesler in a story about Haldane’s habit, identify Haldane’s day and month of birth.
! 5 November.
/ Haldane was a pyromaniac; for example, he liked keeping match and explosives in one pocket. His friends regarded this with fear and suspicion. We do not know what triggered his pyromania, whether it was that traumatic childhood experience with methane, or his birthday falling on the day Guy Fawkes attempted to burn down the English Parliament. The word “remembered” in the question hints at the song “Remember, remember the fifth of November!”
^ 
- Б. Меслер, Д. Кливз. Краткая история сотворения мира (Bill Mesler and H. James Cleaves II, A Brief History of Creation); https://tinyurl.com/yrwe3tpy
- https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/J._B._S._Haldane
- https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Guy_Fawkes
@ Maxim Merzlyakov

? Evgeny Golovin [yev-GAY-ny go-law-VIN] writes that the saying “What a woman wants, God wants” is the inversion of a Latin quote from THIS. THIS is also the title of a death metal album. Identify THIS.
! Malleus Maleficarum [accept Hammer of the Witches]
/ Golovin believes that this saying inverts the original “Feminum luxus, diabolum luxus”, meaning “A woman’s joy is the devil’s joy”, originally a quote from the 15th-century misogynistic treatise Hammer of the Witches. The book was an instruction on identifying and prosecuting witches. Its subject lies close to the interests of death metal bands; for example, its title became the title of an album by Pestilence.
^ 
- Е. Головин. Сентиментальное бешенство рок-н-ролла; https://tinyurl.com/yc8dyttt
- https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Malleus_Maleficarum
- https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Malleus_Maleficarum_(album)
@ Maxim Merzlyakov

? To conceal his identity, a character in a historical mystery novel from the Brother Cadfael series assumes the name Matthew. Interestingly, by the end of novel some unexpected family relations are revealed. What was his real name?
! Luke [accept Lucas].
/ The character, baptised after Luke the Evangelist, found no better trick than to assume the name of another Evangelist, Matthew. Interestingly, in the finale Cadfael admits that he is another character’s father, although not Luke’s, otherwise the parallel to Star Wars would have been too striking.
^ 
- Э. Питерс. Роковой обет (Ellis Peters, The Pilgrim of Hate); https://tinyurl.com/y75dnsw6
- https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/The_Empire_Strikes_Back
@ Maxim Merzlyakov

? Brother Cadfael left the secular world to become a monk, but he still had deep affection for a certain Winifred, who he called “Girl”. What word was omitted in the previous sentence?
! The.
/ This detail is likely an allusion to Sherlock Holmes, for whom “The Woman” was Irene Adler. However, in accordance with his era, Cadfael’s “The Girl” is a Welsh holy martyr, whose bones he helped to unearth and then was filled with love for her.
^ https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Cadfael
@ Maxim Merzlyakov

? A certain musical by Jonathan Larson is largely autobiographical. Its main female character dies of AIDS. The first production of this musical took place in 1996, exactly 100 years after THIS. Identify THIS.
! the premiere of La Boheme [accept all answers that mention La Boheme or its first production specifically, without any wrong additions].
/ The musical Rent is based on the plot of La Boheme by Puccini. The setting is not Paris, but New York, though no less bohemian, and the characters are bohemian too. The main character dies not of tuberculosis, like in Puccini, but of AIDS, more relevant in the 20th century. The musical premiered exactly 100 years after the first production of the opera.
^ 
- https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Rent_(musical)
- https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/La_bohème
@ Maxim Merzlyakov

? [Note to the reader: do not indicate the italics. If you are showing the question text on a screen, remove the formatting beforehand.]
In a story by Maurice Druon, the Muses, listening to Apollo’s music, heard doves cooing, girls laughing in the fields, the sound of a flail threshing corn, and the wind’s plaintive cries in the reeds. Later Apollo claims to have played THEM. According to one suggestion, THEY were created under the impression made by rural areas around Mantua, where a certain person spent several years. Identify this person.
! (Antonio) Vivaldi.
/ In Druon’s rendition of the myth about the contest between Apollo and Marsius, Aopplo’s music includes sounds belonging to all four seasons. According to one scholarly suggestion, Vivaldi’s Four Seasons concerti were written while he was living and working in Mantua.
^ 
- М. Дрюон. Дневники Зевса (Maurice Druon, Les Mémoires de Zeus); https://tinyurl.com/42u5s947
- https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/The_Four_Seasons_(Vivaldi)
@ Maxim Merzlyakov

? The 1930 Soviet film Sleeping Beauty is set around a production of the eponymous ballet. It goes beyond the theatre scene, and some sequences in it symbolise how the art of ballet remains stagnant despite the illusion of progress. In a sequence filmed as a possible epilogue to the movie, THIS starts moving and gets away. Identify THIS exactly.
! Apollo’s chariot.
/ The film shows the Bolshoi theatre both from within and from without, and the authors make an emphasis on the sculpture of Apollo’s chariot on its façade; it portrays movement, but always stays in place. In one of the epilogue scenes that ultimately didn’t make the cut, the sculptured horses seem to start moving, which symbolises the art awakening from slumber.
^ 
- https://ru.wikipedia.org/wiki/Спящая_красавица_(фильм,_1930)
- Д. Хитрова, Ю. Цивьян. Конец фильма; https://tinyurl.com/yhurmt8b
@ Maxim Merzlyakov

? Daria Khitrova [khit-RAW-va] writes that a film director may in some cases sacrifice its ending in order to adapt the product to different conditions. She compares the structure of a film to the anatomy of WHAT?
! lizard.
/ Khitrova states that a film ending is like a lizard’s tail; in case of emergency it can be cast aside and a new and better one can be grown. Something similar happened to the ending of the Sleeping Beauty film from the preceding question.
^ Д. Хитрова, Ю. Цивьян. Конец фильма; https://tinyurl.com/54fjzfsr
@ Maxim Merzlyakov

## Round 2.

? A zybka is a type of Russian hanging cradle. Even though it’s made to rock from side to side, an old peasant woman used the word zybka when reporting her visit to a big city. What did she call a zybka?
! lift [accept elevator].
/ She seemed to have no other word to describe a thing that hangs on a chain and moves around. So when reminiscing about her visit to Leningrad, the old woman said that she took a ride in a zybka.
^ И. Левонтина. О чём речь; https://tinyurl.com/3zens7e3
@ Maxim Merzlyakov

? Handout:
“…and again she betrayed him, and the Philistines bound him”
The word suspense is derived from the verb suspend in the physical sense of hanging. According to Gilles Deleuze, the art of suspense was mastered by the Austrian, whose quote about the biblical Samson is on the handout. Identify this Austrian.
! (Leopold von Sacher-)Masoch.
/ In Masoch’s works, the submissive victim is always expecting new torment (but doesn’t mind it). Deleuze mentions suspense in both senses, because Masoch’s characters are literally bound, manacled, suspended and crucified. The handout has a quote from Venus in Furs, in which the narrator muses about Samson who also suffered because of the power-hungry Delilah.
^ 
- Leopold von Sacher-Masoch, Venus in Furs; https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/6852/pg6852-images.html
- Ж. Делёз. Представление Захер-Мазоха (Gilles Deleuze, Présentation de Sacher-Masoch); https://tinyurl.com/58uj53u9
- https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Leopold_von_Sacher-Masoch
- https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Japanese_bondage
@ Maxim Merzlyakov

? Russian poet Vyacheslav Ivanov, who spent a long time in Italy, thought that his colleague Alexander Blok’s behaviour was not worthy of his talent. Ivanov dedicated to Blok a poem titled God in an X. In X, one could actually see gods and goddesses, including Venus. Identify what X is, as exactly as possible.
! Lupanar (accept Roman brothel and other answers matching that sense).
/ Ivanov criticised Blok for his lecherous behaviour and dedicated his poem God in a Lupanar specifically to Blok. In lupanars, i.e., Roman brothels there were wall-paintings of gods and goddesses, including Venus, the goddess of love.
^ 
- https://ru.wikipedia.org/wiki/Иванов,_Вячеслав_Иванович
- В. Иванов. Бог в лупанарии; https://tinyurl.com/492xr8wd
- https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Lupanar
- https://tinyurl.com/a6hsba58
@ Maxim Merzlyakov

? Carl Zimmer contrasts the artificially modified K12 strain of E. coli with its wild strains and compares K12 to THIS. The author of this question notes that this comparison would be even more appropriate if the strain had a slightly different name. Identify this proposed name and identify THIS using only one word.
! K9, dog(s) [accept: K9, canine(s), or in reverse order].
/ The wild strains that are potentially dangerous for humans, are compared to wolves, while the “domesticated” modified K12 strain used in lab studies is compared to dogs. The author thinks that an appropriate name for the “tame” strain would be K9, with an even closer association to dogs.
^ 
- https://tinyurl.com/2yu3b7pw
- К. Циммер. Микрокосм. E. coli и новая наука о жизни (Carl Zimmer, Microcosm: E. coli and the new science of life); https://tinyurl.com/276tm45y
- https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/K9
- The author’s own opinion; https://tinyurl.com/5yxeyx9v
@ Maxim Merzlyakov

? The entrepreneur John Templeton was bitter about the fact that many ignored the spiritual pursuit that represents another way to progress. In a document published about five decades ago, it is specifically stipulated that the Templeton X should be larger than ANOTHER X. Identify ANOTHER X.
! Nobel Prize.
/ The Templeton Prize has been awarded since 1972 for discoveries and advances in spiritual life, and the laureate can be of any religion or even an atheist. Through the years among the laureates were Mother Teresa and the Dalai Lama, but also, for example, the scientist Freeman Dyson. The prize charter specifically states that its size in money shall always be greater than that of the Nobel Prize.
^ https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Templeton_Prize
@ Maxim Merzlyakov

? A hundred years ago, professors half-jokingly complained that they would teach THIS theory on Mondays, Wednesdays, and Fridays, and THAT one on Tuesdays, Thursdays, and Saturdays. Identify THIS and THAT with one word each.
! particle, wave [accept in any order, also with “quantum” and “corpuscle” instead of “particle”].
/ The theory in question is the theory of light, which baffled scientists with its dual wave-particle nature. Physicists joked that they would teach the wave theory 3 days a week and the particle theory 3 other days.
^ 
- https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Wave–particle_duality
- М. Кумар. Квант (Manjit Kumar. Quantum); https://tinyurl.com/5n6mh2dr
@ Maxim Merzlyakov

? Initially it was thought that a black hole can only consume matter and energy. But after the emergence of Hawking’s radiation hypothesis, physicists started going as far as to mention the black hole mass effect. In the previous sentence, certain words have been substituted. Restore them.
! half-life.
/ Hawking suggested that black holes can degrade and lose mass this way. As an analogy to elementary particles, the idea of half-life emerged, which depends on the initial mass and the intensity of radiation. The substitution uses titles of videogame series, Half-Life and Mass Effect.
^ 
- https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Hawking_radiation
- https://tinyurl.com/ytenwmvn
- https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Half-life
- https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Mass_Effect
- https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Half-Life_(video_game)
@ Maxim Merzlyakov

? In the beginning of a famous novel, the main character tries to stand up from a chair but sits back down immediately to avoid embarrassment. Later, when the situation becomes even more frightening, the character mentiones a “monstrous eraser”. Identify this novel.
! Thinner.
/ The character’s trousers were about to fall from him, so he was forced to sit back down immediately. However, his troubles were only starting; later, realising he was losing weight drastically, he compared this to a monstrous eraser deleting his kilograms.
^ С. Кинг. Худеющий (Stephen King, Thinner); https://tinyurl.com/2fj2b83p
@ Maxim Merzlyakov

? [Note to the reader: do not indicate the presence of the quotation marks. If you are showing the question text on a screen, delete them beforehand.]
The creators of Serutan, a cellulose-based laxative, set it against its synthetic alternatives. In its marketing slogan, people were asked to do something to “serutan”. What were they asked to do?
! read backwards [accept any answer that means the same, like “spell backwards, pronounce in reverse” etc.]
/ Serutan was positioned as a natural medicine, and its slogan was “Read it backwards”. When read this way, it becomes “Nature’s”.
^ https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Serutan
@ Maxim Merzlyakov

? [Note to the reader: do not indicate the italics. If you are showing the question text on a screen, remove the formatting beforehand.]
In a book by Pascal Boyer, there is a suggestion that a particular type of THIS can be caused by foods that potentially impede development. THIS is set in a town called Bouville which can be translated as Mudtown. Identify THIS.
! Nausea.
/ In pregnancy, nausea and vomiting can be caused by ordinary foods. According to one hypothesis, this type of nausea is an evolutionary mechanism that protects the mother’s body from toxins that can impede fetal development. The prototype for the disgusting town of Bouville in Sartre’s novel Nausea was Le Havre, where the author taught philosophy.
^ 
- П. Буайе. Объясняя религию. Природа религиозного мышления (Pascal Boyer, Religion Explained: The Evolutionary Origins of Religious Thought); https://tinyurl.com/ysdnj5wh
- https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Nausea_(novel)
@ Maxim Merzlyakov

? After an unethical experiment, the physician Roberts Bartolow got attacked by outraged colleagues. Which Latin-based adjective did Sam Keane use in his book to describe this reaction?
! autoimmune.
/ An autoimmune reaction is an attack on one’s own body cells by one’s immune system. Keane compares the medical community to the immune system: it did everything to expel Bartolow from its ranks after an inhumane experiment on a patient’s brain.
^ 
- С. Кин. Дуэль нейрохирургов (Sam Keane, The Tale of the Dueling Neurosurgeons); https://tinyurl.com/er3axchk
- https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Autoimmune_disease
@ Maxim Merzlyakov

? In this question, X and Y stand for specific words. According to the philosopher Hegel, a falling brick is deadly because of its speed, so if a human is killed by it, it is actually killed by X and Y. The compound word made of X and Y often precedes a noun that comes from Latin. Which noun is it?
! continuum.
/ X and Y stood here for “space” and “time”. Hegel’s idea was that it’s not the brick itself that is deadly, but rather the energy it acquires due to travelling in space and time. The combined notion of spacetime is also specifically called “spacetime continuum”. And we will continue our game after a break.
^ 
- Г. Гегель. Энциклопедия философских наук (Georg Wilhelm Friedrich Hegel, Enzyklopädie der philosophischen Wissenschaften); https://tinyurl.com/y8s3h4yk
- https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Spacetime
- https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/continuum#English
@ Maxim Merzlyakov

## Round 3.

? [Note to the reader: do not signal the italics. If you are showing the question text on a screen, remove the formatting beforehand.]
One character in Dickens’s Little Dorrit was seen as an economics genius but ended up bankrupting thousands. Bernard Shaw believed Little Dorrit impacts the reader more than THIS. In the USSR, Sergei Eisenstein was planning a screen adaptation of THIS, while German theatre directors made a play based on THIS. Identify THIS.
! Capital [accept Das Kapital].
/ Shaw thought that Dickens’s novel is better at promoting revolutions and the downfall of capitalism than Capital by Karl Marx. Capital is not known for rigid research, and the author himself called it a literary work, so it makes sense to adapt it in theatre and film.
^ 
- https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Little_Dorrit
- https://tinyurl.com/2p8v93b9
- https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Das_Kapital
@ Maxim Merzlyakov

? John Greenleaf Whittier grew up in the early 19th century on a farm. As a child, he got a lot of free time to study poetry, because he could not help during harvest due to THIS. In many European languages, THIS is named after an older contemporary of Whittier. Who was that other person?
! (John) Dalton.
/ Due to colour-blindness, Whittier could not distinguish ripe and unripe berries, the leisure time that he got was used for reading, and this helped him become a prominent poet. The condition was described in the late 18th century by John Dalton, using himself as a case study.
^ 
- https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/John_Greenleaf_Whittier
- https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/John_Dalton
@ Maxim Merzlyakov

? In the 13th century, at a festivity in the town of Treviso, girls were defending a “love castle” from boys. In one description we read that the girls fought with Xs, and the boys with Ys, and as a result they all got scratched. Identify X or Y, using only two words.
! white rose [or “red rose”].
/ The girls’ weapons were white roses, symbols of chastity, and the boys had red ones, symbols of passion. This was another “War of the roses”, two hundred years before the English one.
