- https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Baseball_metaphors_for_sex
- https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Baseball
@ Maxim Merzlyakov

? John Olsson writes that THEIR telltale sign is that THEY are forced to use very rare synonyms. For example, THEY would describe a rich interior as lavish instead of luxury. Identify THEM, using a word of Latin origin.
! Plagiarists.
/ Olsson’s book deals with linguistic text analysis, for example, finding evidence of plagiarism. He observes that plagiarists usually try to “copy with variations” and substitute individual words with synonyms. The original text would typically use the more common words for things, and so plagiarists are forced to use obscure words.
^ 
- Д. Олссон. Слово как улика (John Olsson, Forensic Linguistics); https://tinyurl.com/2pv9hcmr
- https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Plagiarism
@ Maxim Merzlyakov

? To preserve secrecy, the Swedish Academy uses codenames for nominees to the Nobel Prize. The British writer Doris Lessing, once a member of the Communist party, got a two-word codename. Identify this codename.
! Little Dorrit.
/ Her first name sounded like the Dickens character’s surname, and her surname had “less” in it. Moreover, she used to be a Communist, and you already know from our questions that this novel by Dickens has ideas that lie close to Communism.
^ 
- https://tinyurl.com/2p872brv
- https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Doris_Lessing
@ Maxim Merzlyakov

? THEY first appeared in 1948, and at first they differed from the neighbours’ version only by an overprint. THEY feature a quote from Prophet Muhammad: “Seeking an honest livelihood is an act of worship”. Identify THEM using only two words.
! Pakistani rupees.
/ Pakistani rupees were introduced soon after the country’s independence, but at first they were just Indian Rupees with “Government of Pakistan” overprinted on them. On some rupee notes, the translation of a Muslim hadith on money can be found.
^ https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Pakistani_rupee
@ Maxim Merzlyakov

? During the conquest of Siberia, Russians faced stern resistance from the Chukchi [CHOOK-chi], a people from the far north. One of their customs had, among others, the function of intimidation. What designation, known since late 3rd century, can be explained by a similar custom?
! Picts.
/ Chukchi face tattoos served, among others, the purpose of striking fear into their enemies. Russians even called them “paint-faces”. The Roman Empire, during its conquest of the British Isles, also faced tribes from the north that had that same custom. So Romans called these tribes picti — “the painted ones”. Both Picts and Chukchi resisted the invaders fiercely.
^ 
